### Page 4 of 5

```text
Planning Commission
Minutes of the Proceedings - Final
June 27, 2024
Shane Hansen, applicant's agent, was present to answer questions.
Chairman Caughey opened the Public Hearing. Speaking in opposition to the zone
change were Leona Lowe, Jeff Reif, Clint Taylor, and Joanne Greenwalt. Concerns
raised were increase in traffic, commercial development is not wanted, decreased
property valuations, does not want multi-family development, and keep neighborhood
as is. Hearing no further comments Chairman Caughey closed the hearing and called
for a motion and discussion.
Jack Stadel said that he is not aware of any multi-family projects approved in the
county unless it was connected to a public water and sewer system. It is almost
impossible to meet all the requirements for multi-family with a private well and septic
system.
Jack Stadel moved, seconded by Mark Smith to recommend approval of
the Zone Change from AR to MU for Tract 2, Church of Christ
Subdivision, 1st Filing and Tract 2, Church of Christ Subdivision, 2nd
Filing to the Laramie County Board of Commissioners with one
condition.Vote 3-0.
Caughey, Stadel, Smith
Aye:
Gaspar
Excused:
Watkins
Recused:
PUBLIC HEARING for the review and action of a Variance for the
"Williams Street Modular Home" setback requirement to three feet, six
inches on the rear side of the tract.
24 - 332
PZ-24-00055 PC Meeting Packet
Attachments:
Cate Cundall, Associate Planner, said that 210 Properties, LLC, submitted an
application seeking approval of a rear yard variance for a manufactured home. They
are requesting a 16.5' setback variance to allow the structure to be set 3.5 feet from
the rear yard lot line. There is an existing fifteen foot easement on the east side of the
lot that makes the lot too narrow for angling the trailer to allow it to sit within the
setbacks on all four sides.
The use or enjoyment of adjacent properties would not be affected as the proposed
variance would match or exceed the setback distances of adjacent properties. The
structure to the north of the subject property appears to have no setback distance from
the back of the property. Approval of the variance would not harm the public safety and
welfare nor alter the essential character of the neighborhood. It would be substantially
equivalent to the other landowners in the neighborhood, which are grandfathered as
non-conforming. The variance applicance meets all the criteria for granting a variance.
